{"episodes":{"189020":"A large upper low pressure system approached the central California coast on April 13 spreading increased clouds and precipitation into the interior of central California during the afternoon while temperatures plummeted as a cooler airmass pushed into the area. The most noticeable impact rom this system was strong wind gusts which accompanied the main cold front. Several reports of wind gusts exceeding 45 mph were reported during the early afternoon of April 13 while a few stations reported gusts above 60 mph. There were several law enforcement reports of trees downed in the San Joaquin Valley. This system also produced widespread precipitation across the area as the low moved across the area with several stations picking up between a half inch and an inch of liquid precipitation. The precipitation mainly fell as snow above 5000 feet and several higher elevation SNOTELs in the Sierra Nevada picked up an estimated 4 to 8 inches of new snowfall. The precipitation continued until the afternoon of April 14 when showers tapered off as the upper low moved to the east of the area.","189026":"A cold front dropped southward across Central Pennsylvania during the afternoon and evening hours of April 14, 2024, accompanied by a line of showers and storms. The storms developed in a relatively dry environment, with plentiful shear and adequate CAPE. Damaging winds were the primary issue, although there were two reports of quarter-sized hail. The storms weakened as they moved into southern Pennsylvania late in the evening.","189251":"A cold front crossing the area sparked a line of thunderstorms that moved into Northeast PA late in the afternoon and evening on the 14th. Enough instability was in place to lead to a few severe thunderstorms capable of producing hail and damaging wind gusts over the Wyoming Valley.","189709":"Showers and thunderstorms developed on the afternoon of April 11th due to the combination of unseasonably warm temperatures and a passing disturbance. Storms crossed in from the Ohio River Valley during the early afternoon and continued an eastward progression into the evening. A swath of tree damage and power outages were observed from Mingo County to the eastern half of the West Virginia from damaging wind gusts. Storms pressed eastward late on the evening of April 11th.||In addition, numerous areas of flooding transpired that afternoon and evening in response to heavy downpours and training storms throughout the state. The Charleston airport set records for the wettest April as a result of observing 3.32 inches of rainfall on April 11th, breaking the old record of 1.83 inches set back in 1965.||High water issues along state roads were the main impacts from the flooding, some of which needed water rescues for cars that drove into these trouble spots. Excess water eventually drained into the Ohio River on the 12th and 13th of April, returning to its banks a few days later. One fatality occurred in Wood County on the 14th, when a motorist drove past high water signs and their vehicle became submerged.","189731":"A line of convection developed along passing cold front and trough during the late afternoon of April 14th over Lake Erie. The line matured and slowly progressed south and created severe hail and flooding due to training. Effective shear was maximized between 18Z and 00Z over northern PA with a low-level jet originated from west to east.  Surface instability likely ranged from 500J/kg to 1000J/kg and shear from the surface to 500mb likely ranged between 45kt to 55kts.","189820":"At the surface, a low moved southeastward from western Lake Ontario to near Long Island Sound during the early afternoon through late evening of the 14th. Simultaneously, the trailing cold front swept southward from southern portions of ON, MI, and Lake Michigan, respectively, to central portions of IN, OH, and PA, respectively. Along and ahead of the front, scattered multicell thunderstorms developed and persisted generally eastward or southeastward across northeast OH during the late afternoon through early evening. The storms produced hail, some of which was severe, in several counties, and a severe peak convective wind gust in Trumbull County. The storms' ambient environment exhibited weak to moderate MUCAPE, steep midlevel lapse rates near 7C-8C/km, moderate DCAPE, and moderate to strong effective bulk shear.","189874":"An area of showers and thunderstorms rapidly developed across north-central Wisconsin during the late evening hours on Saturday (4/13) near a semi-stationary front.  The strongest storms produced small hail up to penny size as they tracked through the area.","190004":"A strong cold front approached from the northwest, moving into a very warm and moist airmass. This resulted in some scattered strong to severe thunderstorms over eastern Pennsylvania, bringing damaging winds to portions of the state.","190071":"An advancing low pressure system entered into the Ohio Valley on April 11th and set forth strong thunderstorms and heavy downpours. Storms tracked through initially early in the afternoon, stirring up strong winds that blew down a tree near the town of Ironton. As multiple rounds of showers and storms pressed across Ohio, heavy downpours over already saturated soils drained into the local creeks and streams and resulted in river flooding beginning that evening. Flooding eventually stemmed into the Ohio River on the 13th as water flowed down from New York and Pennsylvania, where heavy rain also occurred. Several gauges along the Ohio River from Willow Island Lock, Marietta, and Pomeroy rose above flood stage, resulting in flooded streets in the nearby towns. The river returned below flood at all three locations on April 14th and 15th.","190815":"Thunderstorms developed over Wallowa County during the afternoon and early evening hours of April 14, producing reports of severe hail over the area."},"events":[{"begin":"2024-04-14T01:17Z","county":"Kings","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T03:17Z","endLat":36.2271,"endLon":-119.5995,"episode":189020,"id":1162479,"injuries":0,"lat":36.23,"location":"1.81 ENE Guernsey","lon":-119.6,"narrative":"California Highway Patrol reported flooding on SR 43 north of Kent Ave.","source":"Law Enforcement","state":"CA","type":"Flood","wfo":"HNX"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T02:03Z","county":"Washington","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-15T08:07Z","endLat":39.4354,"endLon":-81.4314,"episode":190071,"id":1171706,"injuries":0,"lat":39.4061,"location":"1.19 ENE Williamstown","lon":-81.4291,"narrative":"The Ohio River at Marietta exceeded its flood stage on the night of April 13th and continued to rise to a crest of around 37.9 feet overnight. After cresting, the river slowly fell and returned below flood stage early on the 15th. Several local roads in Marietta flooded as a result during this time, including Hart Street, Jefferson Street, and Pike Street.","source":"River/Stream Gage","state":"OH","type":"Flood","wfo":"RLX"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T02:31Z","county":"Oneida","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T02:32Z","endLat":45.86,"endLon":-89.69,"episode":189874,"id":1167048,"injuries":0,"lat":45.86,"location":"0.84 SE Minocqua","lon":-89.69,"magnitude":0.75,"narrative":"Penny size hail fell in Minocqua.","source":"Public","state":"WI","type":"Hail","wfo":"GRB"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T04:45Z","county":"Meigs","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-15T16:15Z","endLat":39.0376,"endLon":-82.0205,"episode":190071,"id":1171707,"injuries":0,"lat":39.0299,"location":"1.16 E Pomeroy","lon":-82.0084,"narrative":"The gauge along the Ohio River at Pomeroy indicated a rise above flood stage on the night of April 13th. The river crested around 47 feet the next afternoon and then fell back below flood stage late in the morning on the 15th. While above flood stage, parking lots in Pomeroy and the river amphitheater flooded, as well as secondary roads due to backwater.","source":"River/Stream Gage","state":"OH","type":"Flood","wfo":"RLX"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T21:02Z","county":"Mercer","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T21:02Z","endLat":41.36,"endLon":-80.46,"episode":189731,"id":1169456,"injuries":0,"lat":41.36,"location":"2.59 NW Transfer","lon":-80.46,"magnitude":0.75,"narrative":"Nickel sized hail.","source":"Public","state":"PA","type":"Hail","wfo":"PBZ"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T21:11Z","county":"Trumbull","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T21:11Z","endLat":41.3903,"endLon":-80.8674,"episode":189820,"id":1166780,"injuries":0,"lat":41.3903,"location":"0.72 N Bristolville Arpt","lon":-80.8674,"magnitude":1.5,"narrative":"Hail up to ping pong ball-size in Bristolville.","source":"Public","state":"OH","type":"Hail","wfo":"CLE"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T21:12Z","county":"Mercer","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T21:12Z","endLat":41.35,"endLon":-80.08,"episode":189731,"id":1169457,"injuries":0,"lat":41.35,"location":"Sandy Lake","lon":-80.08,"magnitude":0.75,"narrative":"Nickel sized hail reported.","source":"Trained Spotter","state":"PA","type":"Hail","wfo":"PBZ"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T21:14Z","county":"Geauga","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T21:14Z","endLat":41.3707,"endLon":-81.0206,"episode":189820,"id":1166772,"injuries":0,"lat":41.3707,"location":"2.56 E Parkman","lon":-81.0206,"magnitude":1.0,"narrative":"Hail up to the size of quarters about 2.6-miles east of Parkman.","source":"NWS Employee","state":"OH","type":"Hail","wfo":"CLE"},{"begin":"2024-04-14T21:18Z","county":"Mercer","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-04-14T21:18Z","endLat":41.33,"endLon":-80.14,"episode":189731,"id":1169535,"injuries":0,"lat":41.33,"location":"1.04 W Stoneboro","lon":-80.14,"magnitude":56.0,"magn